  "slug": "linked-list-cycle-ii",
  "title": "Linked List Cycle II",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Fast slow pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "fast & slow pointers",
    "cycle detection",
    "linked list"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "first, can you even tell if there's a cycle at all? that's a smaller version of this same problem",
    "run fast (2 steps) and slow (1 step) pointers from the head until they meet somewhere inside the cycle - if fast reaches the end instead, there's no cycle, return -1",
    "once they meet, there's a neat trick: the distance from the head to the cycle's start equals the distance from the meeting point back around to the cycle's start",
    "reset one pointer to the head, keep the other at the meeting point, and move both one step at a time - the node where they meet again is the start of the cycle"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "returning the meeting point of fast/slow instead of continuing to find the actual cycle start",
    "not handling pos = -1 as no-cycle and instead returning some index",
    "forgetting to reset one pointer to the head before the second phase of the walk"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A singly linked list is described by an array `nums`, where `nums[i]`\nis the value of the i-th node and node i normally points to node i+1.\nYou're also given an integer `pos`: the 0-indexed position that the\nlast node's next pointer connects back to, instead of ending the list\nnormally. Reason about the list as if traversing it with a next\npointer that wraps around to index `pos` after the last element when\n`pos != -1`.\n\n**Return the 0-indexed position in `nums` where the cycle begins, or -1\nif there is no cycle.**\n\nIf `pos` is -1, the list has no cycle.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "-1 <= pos < nums.length",
      "1 <= nums.length <= 10^4"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "pos = -1 (no cycle, return -1)",
      "cycle begins at the head (pos = 0)",
      "single-node list that cycles to itself (pos = 0)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
